  "summary": "EFF advises homeowners' associations and neighborhoods against installing ALPRs like Flock, arguing they don't reduce crime, enable neighbor and police surveillance abuses, produce dangerous false hits, and lack oversight. The piece cites a California State Auditor report and incidents of misidentification to support its concerns.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "despite what ALPR vendors like Flock Safety and Vigilant Solutions claim, there is no real evidence that ALPRs reduce crime",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "a false alert from an ALPR led police to stop a Black family, point guns at them, and force them to lie on their bellies in a parking lot",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "a state audit in Vermont found that 11% of ALPR searches violated state restrictions",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock, a vendor that aims to offer this technology to neighborhoods, has just announced a new nationwide partnership that allows communities to share footage and data with law enforcement anywhere in the country",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    }
  ],
